<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Parsua</h1><p class="st-meta">843 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Zagros land of many small chiefdoms first named in 843 BCE, whose name may be the earliest mention of the Persians.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Parsua (Parsuash) is first recorded in 843 BCE in the annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, who in 835 BCE received tribute from 27 kings of Parsua. It lay in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>, probably near the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Diyala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Parsua was not a unified kingdom but a cluster of chiefdoms under local lords who paid horses and livestock to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. The similarity of the name to Parsa, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, has led many historians to connect Parsua with early Iranian groups who later settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, but whether the same people moved south, or the name simply meant 'borderland', is debated.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tiglath-Pileser III turned Parsua into an Assyrian province in 744 BCE, and it remained in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> until the rise of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, rulers of the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-anshan">Kingdom of Anshan</a>, may preserve its name.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/parsua.jpg" alt="Parsua" width="320" height="532"><figcaption>Image: Hadanish · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parsua.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>843 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Parsuash</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-772&amp;focus=parsua" aria-label="Parsua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6098 1025 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Parsua lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6418 1281h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6418 1281h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iran. <a href="/atlas?year=-772&amp;focus=parsua">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

